      "id": "lpic1-swap-prepared-activation",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-1",
      "topic": "Swap activation and saved policy",
      "unit": "linux-swap-policy",
      "title": "Make prepared swap available now and next time",
      "summary": "Distinguish prepared space, saved instructions and actual active swap while preserving data.",
      "objective": "fast-swap is the supplied prepared area UUID 00006100-0000-4000-8000-000000000003. Keep it active with priority 40 and automatically selected at that priority by the saved policy. Keep reserve active at its original priority 5, but manual (noauto) in the saved file. An active reserve need not be automatically selected. Keep /data mounted writable and /audit mounted read-only with their original UUIDs, automatic saved policies and supplied final fields. The saved configuration must retain these choices when the supplied device names are permuted. Use supported literal commands and the virtual editor only.",
      "retry_guidance": "Read Saved and Active feedback separately. Inspect the active table before repeating an activation; keep protected mounts and the reserve intact.",
      "difficulty": "Beginner",
      "minutes": 15,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Repair saved policy and inspect active areas",
        "Apply only the needed changes and verify"
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "A swap signature prepares space, a saved automatic row defines future selection, and activation supplies the present state. All three differ. Stable UUIDs keep saved policy tied to the intended area; noauto is not an instruction to deactivate an existing reserve.",
      "hints": [
        "Compare the supplied UUID with the saved source and the NAME/PRIO rows; a saved policy is not an active-state report.",
        "Change only the requested swap row(s). Keep the complete original /data and /audit policy and their live mounts.",
        "After saving an automatic swap row with the required priority, bulk activation reads it. A manual reserve is skipped and remains at its existing active priority."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-101-102-objectives/",
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-201-202-objectives/",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/swapon.8.html",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/fstab.5.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Source-enabled prepared partitions only; no host devices, paging, memory/OOM policy, swap files, zram/zswap, discard, hibernation, kernel-selected priorities or full utility semantics. Only six literal fields and defaults/auto/noauto/one explicit pri are accepted. Real swapon can ignore unknown options; this simulator refuses them honestly. It validates complete supported syntax before bulk activation; admitted row failures preserve other successes. Fresh-policy cases permute supplied aliases in independent in-memory copies; they are not boot, init/systemd or physical-device acceptance.",
      "initial": {
        "kind": "PersistentMounts",
        "storage": {
