      "storage_teaching": {
        "prerequisite_note": "Builds on prepared partitions and saved/live mount practice. This lesson teaches swap activation and explicit priority; no Linux swap command runs on your computer.",
        "concepts": [
          {
            "title": "Prepared, saved and active are different states",
            "text": "Swap is space the kernel can use to hold memory pages outside RAM. This activity models prepared partition identities and activation only; it does not page memory or change this computer. Earlier disk preparation supplied the signature. A prepared area is not necessarily active, and a saved row is an instruction rather than proof of activation."
          },
          {
            "title": "Read the supplied identity and the active table",
            "text": "Use blkid to match each supplied UUID and its current /dev alias. The UUID identifies the same area if device names change. Run swapon --show --bytes to inspect active areas and their PRIO values. SIZE is supplied usable bytes; USED stays zero because this activity has no paging workload. It is not a host memory measurement."
          },
          {
            "title": "Write a swap policy row",
            "text": "A swap row has six fields: source, none, swap, options, 0, 0. The target none is unused; swap is not mounted on a directory. Here pri=N gives an explicit priority from 0 to 32767; higher numbers are preferred. Auto selects bulk activation; noauto skips it but still permits an explicit request. For swapon -a, remove noauto when you want automatic activation; adding auto later does not cancel it. This bounded subset does not implement negative kernel defaults, swap files or other swap options."
          },
          {
            "title": "Save policy, then change active state",
            "text": "Type the complete policy in the editor and choose Save configuration. That changes saved text only. Run swapon -a to activate supported automatic rows; it skips noauto and already-active areas. Alternatively swapon -p N -U UUID explicitly activates one prepared area. No command runs when you fill the worked example. Keep the existing data mounts and reserve area unchanged."
          },
          {
            "title": "Verify both the present and the next policy evaluation",
            "text": "Check configuration grades saved and active facts separately. Fresh-policy checks use independent copies with supplied aliases changed; they are not a real reboot and do not replace your current state. A correct current /dev name can select a different area in that test. Inspect the specific failed rows, repair only the mistake and check again."
          }
        ],
        "example_description": "The separate workshop host uses UUID 00006200-0000-4000-8000-000000000003. Its desired automatic/active priority is 30; standby stays manual and active at priority 5. The workshop signature already exists but its area is not active. Preserve both supplied data mounts.",
