        "prerequisite_note": "Builds on prepared partitions and saved/live mount practice. This lesson teaches swap activation and explicit priority; no Linux swap command runs on your computer.",
        "concepts": [
          {
            "title": "Read the handover as three checks",
            "text": "The earlier activity separated prepared, saved and active areas. This handover adds a replacement and a retirement. Inspect the intended replacement, the obsolete area and the protected reserve separately; a correct command for one does not prove the whole handover."
          },
          {
            "title": "The saved file can be ahead of active state",
            "text": "In the workshop example, the saved replacement row requests priority 90 while swapon --show --bytes still reports 10. That active row records the earlier activation. Repeating swapon -a skips an already-active area; it cannot adopt the new saved priority."
          },
          {
            "title": "Retirement needs both policy and deactivation",
            "text": "Removing the old saved row prevents future automatic selection, but does not stop its current activation. Deactivating it without removing that row leaves it able to return in the next policy test. Check both facts without changing standby or either data mount."
          },
          {
            "title": "Apply the desired priority to the intended area",
            "text": "Save the desired policy, deactivate only the obsolete and stale replacement areas, then activate the desired automatic row. Match UUIDs and aliases before each request; standby must retain its own active priority. The supplied areas have no paging workload. Real swapoff can fail if memory is insufficient; that runtime behavior is not modeled."
          },
          {
            "title": "Verify future identity without resetting the session",
            "text": "Test saved policy compares expected and actual automatic areas in independent copies with device aliases changed. Stable UUIDs keep the plan tied to the right area. An empty plan, an active-only repair or a stale old row fails a different requirement; read the specific feedback and retry only that part."
          }
